Mikhail,

I've been thinking at your issue, and here's what I thought : you only 
have average phenotype of the offspring of a sire, right ? If I 
understand correctly, this means you already have your sire effect ! 
What I mean by this is that, by taking the average, you get rid of the 
residual variations. So if you withdraw the mean phenotype from each 
value (which already is the average value by sires), the rest should be 
the sire effect.

This way, the variance of your phenotypic values are directly sire 
variance. However, you don't have access to the real phenotypic variance 
(Vp), which is necessary to estimate heritability... Maybe you could 
estimate the residual variance (or the within-family variance) from a 
family subset ?

Excuse-me if I didn't understand correctly your experimental design...

Cheers,
Pierre.

